## Section: Cases

<section id="cases">
<purpose>Explain what cases ARE and when to use each</purpose>

<intro>
In English, word order shows meaning: "The dog bit the man" vs "The man bit the dog."
In Greek, word **endings** show meaning, so word order is flexible. These endings are called **cases**.
</intro>

<hero title="The Four Questions" colour="ocean">
Ask these questions to know which case to use. Notice how Γιάννης changes form in each:

| Case | Question | Example | Translation |
|------|----------|---------|-------------|
| Nominative | Who/what does? | Ο Γιάννης τρέχει | Giannis runs |
| Accusative | Who/what receives? | Βλέπω τον Γιάννη | I see Giannis |
| Genitive | Whose? | Το σπίτι του Γιάννη | Giannis's house |

<guidance>
Focus on Nominative and Accusative first. They cover most of what you'll encounter.
Genitive comes up with possession and some prepositions.
</guidance>

<collapsible title="Vocative (for calling someone)">
| Question | Example | Translation | Note |
|----------|---------|-------------|------|
| Hey...! | Γιάννη! | Hey Giannis! | Often same as accusative |
</collapsible>
</hero>

<collapsible title="Quick Spot-Check Patterns" colour="honey">
<patterns>
| Pattern | Meaning | Examples |
|---------|---------|----------|
| -ς ending | Usually nominative masculine | ο φίλος, ο Γιάννης |
| No -ς | Usually accusative masculine | τον φίλο, τον Γιάννη |
| -ου ending | Usually genitive | του φίλου, του Γιάννη |
</patterns>
</collapsible>
</section>

