## Section: Pronouns

<section id="pronouns">
<purpose>The most frequently used words demonstrating cases</purpose>

### Pronoun Decision Guide

<decision-guide title="Which 'me/you' do I use?" colour="honey">
English "me" is one word. Greek splits it by **function**:

| Forms | When to use | Examples |
|-------|-------------|----------|
| με / σε | Someone does something **to** you (direct object) | σε βλέπω (I see you), με ακούς; (do you hear me?) |
| μου / σου | **My/your** (possession) OR **to me/you** (indirect) | το σπίτι μου (my house), μου λέει (tells me) |
| εμένα / εσένα | After prepositions OR for **emphasis** | για μένα (for me), με σένα (with you) |

<cross-comparison title="One English word → multiple Greek forms">
| English | Object | Possessive | Emphatic |
|---------|--------|------------|----------|
| me / my | με | μου | εμένα |
| you / your | σε | σου | εσένα |
| him / his | τον | του | αυτόν |
| her | την | της | αυτήν |
</cross-comparison>

<quick-test>
Can you insert "to" before it? → Use μου/σου.
Is there a preposition before it? → Use εμένα/εσένα.
Otherwise → Use με/σε.
</quick-test>
</decision-guide>

### Pronoun Paradigms

<paradigm-grid>

<paradigm title="Nominative Pronouns" colour="olive" priority="tertiary">
<note>Subject pronouns: often dropped! Verb endings already show person.</note>
<alert variant="success">
Good news: Verb endings already show person, so subject pronouns are usually optional. Use them for emphasis.
</alert>
| Person | Singular | Plural |
|--------|----------|--------|
| 1st | εγώ (I) | εμείς (we) |
| 2nd | εσύ (you) | εσείς (you) |
| 3rd m | αυτός (he) | αυτοί (they m) |
| 3rd f | αυτή (she) | αυτές (they f) |
| 3rd n | αυτό (it) | αυτά (they n) |
<examples>
- εγώ κάνω (I do - emphatic)
- αυτός θέλει (he wants)
</examples>
</paradigm>

<paradigm title="Genitive Pronouns (Possessive)" colour="terracotta" priority="primary" badge="Essential">
<note>For "my/your/their" - goes AFTER the noun</note>
<alert variant="purple">
Word order: Possessives go AFTER the noun: το σπίτι μου (the house my) = my house
</alert>
| Person | Singular | Plural |
|--------|----------|--------|
| 1st | μου (my) | μας (our) |
| 2nd | σου (your) | σας (your) |
| 3rd m | του (his) | τους (their) |
| 3rd f | της (her) | τους (their) |
| 3rd n | του (its) | τους (their) |
<examples>
- το σπίτι μου (my house)
- η μητέρα της (her mother)
- οι φίλοι μας (our friends)
</examples>
</paradigm>

<paradigm title="Accusative Pronouns (Object)" colour="ocean" priority="primary" badge="Essential">
<note>Direct object: use these constantly!</note>
<alert variant="info">
Word order: Object pronouns go BEFORE the verb: σε βλέπω (you I-see) = I see you
</alert>
| Person | Singular | Plural |
|--------|----------|--------|
| 1st | με (me) | μας (us) |
| 2nd | σε (you) | σας (you) |
| 3rd m | τον (him) | τους (them m) |
| 3rd f | την (her) | τις (them f) |
| 3rd n | το (it) | τα (them n) |
<examples>
- σε βλέπω (I see you)
- τον ακούω (I hear him)
- τα θέλω (I want them)
</examples>
</paradigm>

<paradigm title="Emphatic Accusative" colour="honey" priority="tertiary">
<note>Strong forms: after prepositions or for emphasis</note>
<alert variant="warning">
Use these after prepositions (για, με, από, σε): για μένα = for me
</alert>
| Person | Singular | Plural |
|--------|----------|--------|
| 1st | εμένα (me) | εμάς (us) |
| 2nd | εσένα (you) | εσάς (you) |
| 3rd m | αυτόν (him) | αυτούς (them) |
| 3rd f | αυτήν (her) | αυτές (them) |
| 3rd n | αυτό (it) | αυτά (them) |
<examples>
- για μένα (for me)
- με σένα (with you)
- από αυτόν (from him)
</examples>
</paradigm>

</paradigm-grid>

### Key Insight

<insight title="Pronouns = Cases in Action!">
**Accusative** (με, σε, τον) answers "WHOM/WHAT do I [verb]?"
**Genitive** (μου, σου, του) answers "WHOSE?"
You're already using cases every time you use a pronoun!
</insight>

### Double Object Pattern

<pattern title="When you have both indirect and direct object">
Greek puts pronouns BEFORE the verb, indirect first.

| English | Greek | Literal |
|---------|-------|---------|
| Give it to me | Δώσε μου το | Give me-to it |
| Tell me it | Πες μου το | Tell me-to it |
| Show her it | Δείξε της το | Show her-to it |
</pattern>

<collapsible title="Common Phrases with Pronouns">
<phrase-groups>
| Category | Greek | English |
|----------|-------|---------|
| Requests | Πες μου | Tell me |
| Requests | Δώσε μου | Give me |
| Opinions | Μου αρέσει | I like it |
| Opinions | Δεν μου αρέσει | I don't like it |
| Questions | Πώς σε λένε; | What's your name? |
| Answers | Με λένε... | My name is... |
| Family | Η μητέρα μου | My mother |
| Family | Ο πατέρας του | His father |
</phrase-groups>
</collapsible>
</section>

---

## Content Guidelines

1. **Cases first, then pronouns** - Establish the framework before the most common examples
2. **Decision guide prominent** - Most learners need "which form do I use?" help
3. **Mark essential paradigms** - Accusative and genitive are highest frequency
4. **Show word order** - Greek pronoun placement differs from English
5. **Connect to practice** - CTA links to /practice/speed?focus=pronouns
